---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout>
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
        <!-- centered -->
        <div class="text-center">
            <p class="text-5xl font-semibold">
                We are...
                <span id="rollingText" class="block text-blue-600"></span>
            </p>
        </div>

        <script is:inline>
            document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
                const words = [
                    { text: "Biologists", color: "text-blue-600" },
                    { text: "Chemists", color: "text-green-600" },
                    { text: "Scientists", color: "text-purple-600" },
                    { text: "Engineers", color: "text-red-600" },
                    { text: "MSBT!", color: "text-yellow-600" },
                ];

                let index = 0;
                const textElement = document.getElementById("rollingText");

                function updateText() {
                    textElement.classList.remove(...textElement.classList);
                    textElement.classList.add(
                        "block",
                        "transition-opacity",
                        "duration-1000",
                        words[index].color,
                    );
                    textElement.style.opacity = 0;

                    setTimeout(() => {
                        textElement.textContent = words[index].text;
                        textElement.style.opacity = 1;
                    }, 100);

                    index++;
                    if (index < words.length) {
                        setTimeout(updateText, 2000);
                    }
                }

                updateText();
            });
        </script>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
            <img
                class="rounded-lg"
                src="/images/about/comp_modeling_team.jpg"
                alt="Computational modeling team smiling."
            />
            <div>
                <div class="text-2xl font-bold">Who We Are</div>
                <div class="text-gray-700 text-xl">
                    The Michigan Synthetic Biology Team provides students the
                    opportunity to gain hands-on experience conducting research,
                    running a lab, engineering biological systems, and more.
                    Each year, we compete in the iGEM competition in Paris.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="my-8">
            <div class="text-2xl font-semibold">What is Synthetic Biology?</div>
            <div class="text-gray-700 text-xl">
                Synthetic Biology combines biology and engineering to design,
                construct, and test biological devices and systems. Combining
                these two disciplines allows us to better understand life and
                use that understanding to benefit society. Synthetic Biology is
                distinct from Genetic Engineering in that the latter only alters
                existing genes, whereas the former utilizes existing, modified,
                and man-made genes to make life perform useful functions not
                normally found in nature. Through synthetic biology, it may be
                possible to solve some of the world's most challenging problems
                such as energy shortage, environmental pollution, and disease.
                However, we must be wary of potential safety and ethical issues
                along the way.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="my-8">
            <div class="text-2xl font-semibold text-right">What is iGEM?</div>
            <div class="text-gray-700 text-xl text-right">
                iGEM is an international undergraduate synthetic biology
                competition. In 2015, iGEM consisted of 259 teams and 2700
                participants from all over the world. Every year, teams
                research, design, construct, and test a novel biological system.
                Students have at their disposal bits of DNA from the iGEM
                Registry of Standard Biological parts to help aid them in
                building their biological “machine”. In addition, students are
                encouraged to build their own parts and develop new techniques
                to make life easier to engineer. Teams also help to increase
                awareness for synthetic biology through public outreach and
                analyze safety concerns as a part of the project design process.
                Projects are wide ranging and are divided into the following
                categories: Environment, Food and Nutrition, Energy,
                Foundational Advance, Health and Medicine, Information
                Processing, Manufacturing, Software Tools, and New Applications.
                Visit the iGem website for more information.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="my-8">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Who Can Join?</h2>

            <!-- Right aligned -->
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-xl">
                Any undergraduate student with an interest in synthetic biology
                and biological engineering is welcome to join! Students will
                become acquainted with the theory and techniques during weekly
                meetings from February to the end of winter semester. We then
                transition to working on the year's project through the spring,
                summer, and following fall semester. The iGEM competition takes
                place in late October. To join, applications start in the middle
                of January!
            </p>

            <a href="/why-join">Join MSBT!</a>
        </div>

        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8 text-center">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Awards</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc ml-5">
                <li>Gold Medal, iGEM 2022</li>
                <li>Gold Medal, Genetic Engineering Competition 2023</li>
                <li>
                    <a
                        class="text-blue-600 underline"
                        href="https://competition.igem.org/sponsorships/impact-grant"
                        >iGEM Impact Grant Recipient 2023</a
                    >
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8 text-center">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">2023 Project</h2>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">
                Point-of-Care rs773902 Genetic Testing
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">
                We are developing an accessible test for a genetic mutation
                affecting blood clotting therapies, using DNA amplification and
                fluorescent markers.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="container text-center">
            <a
                class="mt-4 px-4 py-2 bg-green-600 text-white rounded hover:bg-green-700"
                href="/project">2023 Project Details</a
            >
        </div>
    </div>
</BaseLayout>
